{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neutron stars aren't dark matter--we \nfigured that out a while ago. But new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "research is telling us that they may \nbe dark matter factories. They may  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11520,
    "dDurationMs": 11280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produce the exotic axion, one of the \nmost popular dark matter candidates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22800,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand reality we need to \nunderstand its building blocks. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25760,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "standard model of particle physics \ndoes a great job of describing all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "known particles—the three generations of \nquarks and leptons plus the bosons that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33240,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carry the four fundamental forces plus the \nmass-granting Higgs particle. But there are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37840,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a few holes in the standard model that we \nneed to fill if we want the full picture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43160,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of those holes is the so-called strong \nCP problem. It’s been called one of the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underrated problem in all of physics. Probably \nbecause it’s such an abstract problem that it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53760,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really hard to describe in non-math terms. We gave \nit a shot in this video—but the long-story-short  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60640,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that the strong force is expected to violate \ncharge-parity symmetry—flipping charge and then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doing a mirror reflection of, say, a proton \nor neutron—should not get you back where you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72960,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "started—but in quantum chromodynamics it seems to \ndo exactly that. The most popular way to fix the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80360,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem is to introduce a new quantum field that \neliminates the CP-violating terms in QCD. And with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every new quantum field you get new particles—and \nin this case the particle is called the axion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94760,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Axions are especially popular because \nthey’re a great dark matter candidate—and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99440,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s because they’re extremely elusive \nand they interact electromagnetic field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104200,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only very weakly. We discuss the prospects of \naxionic dark matter in that same previous vid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110560,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But axions can only be dark matter if they \nmake up around 80% of the mass of the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that’s only possible if they were produced \nin pretty ridiculous numbers right after the big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bang. Even if these things don’t solve the dark \nmatter problem, we really want to know if they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125240,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist because of the less famous but still vexing \nstrong CP problem. Oh, and axions are a pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131920,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generic prediction of string theory, so if you \nwant the latter you probably also want the former."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136280,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we’ve trying looking for axions in various \nways, but it’s tricky because of that very weak  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141760,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetic interaction. But axions do have an \nunusual EM interaction that might give them away.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150280,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Axions sometimes do this thing \nwhere they convert into photons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153960,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and photons sometimes convert into axions—in \nboth cases in the presence of strong magnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields. This could have a number of effects, \nlike allowing us to shine a light through a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163840,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solid wall by using magnets to flip \nphotons into axions and back again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168840,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Various experiments to detect this \nphenomenon haven’t found anything,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172480,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but that could be because we can’t generate \nmagnetic fields strong enough. We expect the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rate of conversion between axions and photons to \nbe very sensitive to magnetic field strength—and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more magnetic field is better. But we don’t know \nthe lower limit of that field strength to get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189480,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a detectable signal. The strength of the fields \nused in modern axion detection experiments maxes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196080,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out at about 10 Tesla, and the current world \nrecord magnetic field is 45 Tesla—not bad,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202520,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but piddling compared to what certain space \nmagnets are able to generate. Take magnetars—a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208320,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "type of neutron star that has cranked its magnetic \nfields up to the vicinity of 10 billion Tesla."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214680,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If axions were not produced prodigiously in the \nBig Bang, neutron stars may be our universe’s most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221720,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "efficient axion factories. But that’s only true \nif they meet certain fairly specific conditions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228520,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with the conditions needed to \nproduce axions. First is the strong magnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field—in certain neutron stars we definitely \nhave that—it’s why we chose them in the first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238880,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "place. Second is a prodigious source of \nphotons to convert into axions. Actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244840,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the requirement is a bit more stringent \nthan that. Axions can be produced when we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249120,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have an time-varying electric field that’s \nparallel to a magnetic field. Now a photon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256280,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a single quantum of oscillating electric \nfield, with a perpendicular magnetic field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260960,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that moves with it. If you send photons into \nan external magnetic field, in some cases the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photon’s electric and the external magnetic \nfields line up and you should produce axions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we want a LOT of axions we need a lot \nof photons. The most promising way to do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that with neutron stars is with a large-scale \nelectric field those stars can generate rather  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than individual photons. Early neutron star \nmodels suggested that a very strong electric  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288520,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field should be generated due to the rotation \nof the neutron star relative to its magnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292400,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field. This is related to the effect in \na standard electrical generator, where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297480,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotation of conducting material in a magnetic \nfield generates current. In a neutron star, a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303040,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "component of the electric field generated this way \nshould be in the direction as the magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, we’ll come back to the requirement \nthat this electric field be time-varying.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314520,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First we should take a closer look at \nthis requirement for parallel electric  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318480,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and magnetic field. Why? Because nature \nhates that. Nature does its very best to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resist the electric field becoming parallel \nto the magnetic—which means nature seems to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "want to shut down axion production. \nAnd it does this in a couple of ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335240,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, this electric field we just generated tends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338200,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to rip electric charges from the surface of \nthe neutron star, upon which those changes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342080,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rearrange themselves to neutralize the \nparallel component of the electric field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346140,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "‘\nSecondly, when the strength of electric field gets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349960,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high enough, we expect a phenomenon similar to the \nformation of lightning. Under extreme voltages,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insulating materials can suddenly become \nconductors in a process known as electrical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "breakdown. When charge builds up in storm clouds, \nelectrical breakdown in the air results in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flow of massive currents—aka lightning. Well, \nthe vacuum is a pretty good insulator, but it can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372880,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also break down until extreme voltates—at least \ntheoretically. The result of this vacuum breakdown  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378600,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the production of many pairs of electrons \nand positrons. And in a neutron star, these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383840,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would work to short out the electric field, and so \ncould potentially put an end to axion production."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now nature may want to shut down our axion \nfactory, but neutron stars are so extreme  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they may be able to manage it anyway. The \nregion of extreme magnetic field around the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401240,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutron star is called magnetosphere. \nThere the super-intense magnetic field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407200,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "co-rotate with the star—up to several \nhundred times per second in some cases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The further we get from the neutron star, the \nfaster the magnetic field lines have to sweep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416920,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through space in order to keep up with the rapidly \nspinning surface of the star. At a certain point,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422240,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those magnetic field lines would have to move \nfaster than the speed of light to keep up,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427000,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so they don’t. The field lines lag behind the \nsurface and the field lines in the magnetosphere,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resulting in these helical magnetic \nfields trailing off into space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of which arise from regions around the \nneutron star’s magnetic poles—the polar caps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetic fields that twist like this will \ninduce the flow of currents—that’s Ampere’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449760,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "law. So charged particles get lifted from \nthe magnetosphere at the polar cap region  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and travel along these helical field lines \nand are sprayed into space. Now remember that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461680,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an excess of charged particles can shut \ndown our electric field—whether they are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465880,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles lifted from the surface, or generated \nin the vacuum breakdown. So now we have a way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471280,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drain those particles into space, which means \nwe make be able to rescue our axion factory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476360,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, do we get axions or don’t we? Well, \nbringing together everything I described,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we end up with a hellishly complex process \nthat is very difficult to fully explore with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485920,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just pen and paper. The only way to properly \nfigure this out is to build neutron stars in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our computers with something called \nplasma particle-in-cell simulations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the answer seems to be that nature fails to \nthwart our axion production. In particular, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502400,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacuum breakdown is extremely bursty—an avalanche \nof pair production shorts the parallel electric  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508960,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field temporarily—but then the field switches back \non. This actually leads to exactly what you want  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515000,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for axion production—a rapidly fluctuating \nelectric field. Another really interesting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing is that the electric field oscillations \nare associated with the emission of powerful  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524040,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetic waves. And indeed neutron stars do \nemit such waves in the form of the intense radio  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531080,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jets of pulsars. We’ve never fully understood the \nsource of these jets, and so these new simulations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538160,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may do two things: explain the observed \nradio jets and give us our axion source."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, once generated, the radio photons \nand axions follow markedly different paths.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Radio waves are strongly beamed—they swivel \nwith the magnetic poles in narrow cones,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556200,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leading to the pulsations we observe when the \nbeam sweeps across our line of sight. Axions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562080,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by contrast, can shoot off in \nall direction. They can convert  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565680,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back into radio waves in directions \nway off the standard pulsar beam."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572440,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This actually gets us to a prediction that might \nallow us to test for axion production. If axions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578240,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can lead to radio waves being launched in random \ndirections, not just with the standard pulsar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582680,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beam, then we should expect neutron stars to glow \nover a wide spectrum of radio frequencies at all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589440,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times. And they don't. Some of the most sensitive \nradio telescopes in the world, including Arecibo  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597240,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Puerto Rico, Parkes in Australia, and the \nGreen Bank Telescope in West Virginia have been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "staring at pulsars for the last fifty years and \nthose pulsars are pretty radio-dark besides the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608320,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regular blips of the radio beams. But this \ndarkness has allowed scientists to set some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the strongest constraints on axions to date, \nusing  observations from 27 nearby neutron stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if the axions produced by a neutron \nstar aren’t immediately detectable through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conversion into radio waves, there \nis one more way we can spot them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632360,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Around 90% of axions created by a neutron \nstar are expected to escape into space—with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638160,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some of them being converted into photons \non the way. But the other 10% are created  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643080,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with velocities below the escape velocity \nof the neutron star, which means they fall  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back and end up orbiting the star. And by the \nway, the escape velocity of a neutron star is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653680,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around half the speed of light, so all even \nthe captured axions can be traveling fast.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659960,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These trapped axions build up in this \nincreasingly dense cloud around the star  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664720,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over thousands or millions of years, and that \naxion cloud might have a few observable affects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673080,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1) They might explain another \nmysterious phenomenon called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nulling, where the pulsar’s radio \nemission is temporarily suppressed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681800,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2) they also might result in bright radio \nemission—which I already told you we don’t see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686960,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in bright neutron stars. But the radio light \nfrom these captured axion clouds would have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693400,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a much narrower frequency range that may have \nrendered it undetectable in previous searches."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699440,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3) when a neutron star’s magnetic field eventually \nfades out over 10 to 100s of millions of years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these axion clouds may evaporate in a burst of \nradio emission that may last millions of years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712880,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or may last only a fraction \nof a second. If the latter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "models show that these would bear a striking \nresemblance to the mysterious Fast Radio  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722920,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bursts—distant and stupendously bright radio \nflashes have perplexed astronomers for years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there are a few radio signatures \nthat might reveal this axion cloud,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and radio astronomers are already working to pin \nthese down, while pulsar experts are trying to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model these processes in more sophisticated ways, \nto see if they really do match the observations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for example of nulling phenomenon or fast \nradio bursts. And future radio facilities  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752840,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the Square Kilometer Array may reveal axionic \nradio signatures that are currently undetectable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759520,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should add here that if neutron stars do \nproduce axions they don’t produce enough to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explain all of dark matter. We’d still need a \nlot of axions produced in the early universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769480,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to achieve that. But if axions are even one \ncomponent of dark matter, then having dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter factories in the modern universe could be \npretty helpful for finding and studying the stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781120,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To summarize—axions may or may not exist, but \nif they do then there’s a very good chance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that neutron stars pump them out in enormous \nquantities and that neutron stars are also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792280,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cloaked in clouds of these strange particles, \nand that axions may even explain pulsar radio  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jets and fast radio bursts. That’s a lot of \nifs, but if these ifs work out then the payoff  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is huge—it’ll be a step beyond the standard \nmodel and perhaps will give us at least one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809520,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the components of dark matter. Definitely \nworth keeping our eye on them neutron stars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815000,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see what strange things are being formed \nin those tiny patches of curved spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822400,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS. \nBrilliant is where you learn by doing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with thousands of interactive lessons \nin physics, math, programming, and AI.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant helps build your critical thinking \nskills through problem solving—not memorizing. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837240,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each lesson is filled with hands-on problem \nsolving that let you actually play with concepts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842880,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if you’re into data analysis, Brilliant \nrecently launched a ton of new content in data,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of which uses real-world data to train you \nto see trends and make better-informed decisions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855160,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s designed for learners of any level to \nstart or continue learning data analysis,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860960,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a fully built out suite of new content, from \nBayes’ Theorem to multiple linear regression. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867960,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’ll learn how to parse and visualize massive \ndatasets to make them easier to interpret. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873920,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you’ll gain insight by working with real data  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876200,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sets from sources retail companies, \nonline streaming services and more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880880,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To try everything Brilliant has to \noffer for free for a full 30 days,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visit brilliant.org/spacetime or click on \nthe link in the description. If you go now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ll also get 20% off an \nannual premium subscription."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you know, one of the long standing \nrules of PBS Space Time is that “It’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never aliens,” but the full rule is that \n“It’s never aliens, until it is.” And in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904720,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case you happen to run into aliens and want to \ncommunicate that you’re open to leaving Earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then take a look at our Take Me With \nYou UV Glow T-Shirt and Hoodie. Both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914120,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the t-shirt and hoodie are UV charged, so \nafter being out in the sun they’ll begin to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918640,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "glow which is perfect for signaling aliens in \nthe dark. Because of the UV printing process,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924520,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re only able to print a limited number. \nThe shirts will be on sale until October  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929360,
    "dDurationMs": 29560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "31st. Check out pbsspacetime.com/shop to check \nthem out. There’s a link the in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
